# `!=`, `&&` and `!` together: the fixes applied together still parse.
mode: syntax
---- query
from t | where a!=1&&!b
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..24
  PIPELINE@0..23
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    NAME_REF@5..6
      IDENT@5..6 "t"
    WHITESPACE@6..7 " "
    PIPE@7..8 "|"
    WHITESPACE@8..9 " "
    WHERE_OP@9..23
      IDENT@9..14 "where"
      WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
      BIN_EXPR@15..23
        BIN_EXPR@15..19
          NAME_REF@15..16
            IDENT@15..16 "a"
          NEQ@16..18 "!="
          LITERAL@18..19
            LONG@18..19 "1"
        AMP2@19..21 "&&"
        PREFIX_EXPR@21..23
          BANG@21..22 "!"
          NAME_REF@22..23
            IDENT@22..23 "b"
  WHITESPACE@23..24 "\n"

error[DS0111]: `&&` is not an operator
  --> query:1:20
  |
1 | from t | where a!=1&&!b
  |                    ^^ this is C's "and"
  |
  = help: use `and` to require both conditions

error[DS0111]: `!` is not an operator
  --> query:1:22
  |
1 | from t | where a!=1&&!b
  |                      ^ this is C's "not"
  |
  = help: negate it with `not(...)`: `not(b)`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from t | where a!=1 and not(b)
which parses without errors
